#############################################################################
### Example experiment 8: Schedule Risk Analysis (SRA) with resources
###
###    Note: This is the schedule risk analysis for projects WITH resources
###          = The experiment uses a resource-feasible schedule as a point of reference (generated by priority rules)
###          (it is similar to Experiment 6, using Monte Carlo simulations, but it adds renewable resources)
###
###   Data used in this experiment: 
###     - The experiment makes use of the three projects from Experiments 3 and 4.
###     - The simulated project progress is done with the Monte Carlo simulator with risk classes
###
###   References:
###      (Note that in most of my books, I applied a Schedule Risk Analysis with a critical-path schedule (ignoring resources). 
###       and it was only in 2022 that we performed our very first study extending the sensitivity metrics to 
###       resource-feasible schedules, so the only results available are in  paper (Source 2)).
###
###      Source 1: The resource-based sensitivity metrics are mentioned only in this book:
###      - Title: The Illusion of Control: Project Data, Computer Algorithms and Human Intuition for Project Management and Control
###      - Author: Mario Vanhoucke
###      - Year: 2024
###      - Publisher: Springer
###      - Source: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-31785-9
###
###      Source 2: The study that proposed the resource-based sensitivity metrics:
###      - Title: Using Earned Value Management and Schedule Risk Analysis with resource constraints for project control
###      - Author: Jie Song, Annelies Martens and Mario Vanhoucke
###      - Year: 2022
###      - Publisher: European Journal of Operational Research, 297(2),
###      - Source: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-31785-9
###
###      Source 3: The project data used in this experiment come from this book:
###      - Title: Project Management with Dynamic Scheduling: Baseline Scheduling, Risk Analysis and Project Control
###      - Author: Mario Vanhoucke
###      - Year: 2013
###      - Publisher: Springer
###      - Source: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-40438-2
###        (note that only projects 1 and 2 come from this book)
###
###    Summary details:
###      - The code generates the following sensitivity metrics (identical to Experiment 5 but now the resource-based version):
###         ° Criticality Index (CI): Measures the probability of an activity lying on the critical path
###         ° Sensitivity Index (SI): Indicates how an activity’s duration affects the project’s total duration
###         ° Schedule Sensitivity Index (SSI): Combines CI and SI to assess an activity’s overall risk to the schedule
###         ° Cruciality Index (CRI(t)): Measures the correlation between activity duration variability and project duration variability
###         ° Cruciality Index (CRI(c)): Measures the correlation between activity cost variability and project cost variability
###
###    Download the summary file "Summary_Experiment_8.pdf" or print on screen
###
#############################################################################

# Procedure to select the correct function to load the project data
def get_project_data(projectID):
    if projectID == 1:
        filename = Filefolder + "DS_Book_Table7_1.rcp"
    elif projectID == 2:
        filename = Filefolder + "DS_Book_Table7_2.rcp" 
    elif projectID == 3:
        filename = Filefolder + "mv2.rcp" 
    else:
        raise ValueError("Invalid projectID. Choose 1, 2, or 3.")
    return filename    

